<answer> Therefore, if $y = \arctan x$, then $y + \arctan \frac{1 - \tan y}{1 + \tan y} = \frac{\pi}{4} (1 + \tan 2y)$. Substituting $y = \arctan x$, we have $\arctan x + \arctan \frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \frac{\pi}{4} \left( 1 + \tan 2 \arctan x  \right)$. Using the tangent sum and double angle formulas
\begin{align*}
\tan\left( \frac{\pi}{4} + \arctan\left(\frac{x}{1 + x^2}\right) \right) 
&= \frac{\tan \frac{\pi}{4} + \tan \arctan\frac{x}{1+x^2}}{1 - \tan \frac{\pi}{4} \tan \arctan\frac{x}{1+x^2}} 
& \text{by substitution and tangent sum formulas}
\\ &= \frac{1 + \frac{x}{1+x^2} \cdot \frac{1}{1+x^2}}{1 - \frac{x}{1+x^2} \cdot \frac{1}{1+x^2}} 
&= \frac{1+x^2}{(1+x^2)^2} 
\end{align*}
which implies that
\begin{align*}
\frac{\pi}{4} \left( 1 + \tan 2 \arctan x \right)
&= \arctan \tan \left( \frac{\pi}{4} + \arctan\frac{x}{1+x^2} \right)
& \text{by double angle formula}
\\
&= \frac{\pi}{4} + \arctan\frac{x}{1+x^2} 
&= \arctan \frac{1+x^2}{(1+x^2)^2}. 
\end{align*}
